That's a part of it. However it isn't as hard and fast as not acquire
ANY XPs. Generally there is enough money in the upper level shops, 
the Minetown shops, and upper level vaults to cover protection up to
XL4 or so. So if you need to kill a monster to get out of a tight
spot go ahead a do it. I'm currently running monks and sometimes 
they generate with only 4 PP for spellcasting, which isn't enough
to use the 1st level spell they start with. So I'll deliberately
raise my XL to 2 so I can cast the spell. It has minimal effects
on the strategy, though you will start to get a second class of
monsters generated because of it.

The second part is that your pet is really your protector during your
run. The objective is to "buff" your pet up to its maximum level. If
you use the 'C' command (call) on your pet, it'll give you three
pet sizes:

little dog/kitten
dog/housecat
large dog/large cat

Each corresponds to a pet level, with associated HP and damage for
the pet. At the top large XP level (XL9) the pet has 72 max HP and
can take out most of the monsters you encounter in a bite or two.

Now it takes some time to buff the pet to this level. I usually
do this in the main dungeon in the top 4 levels or so. Simply
leave all the jackals, grid bugs, newts, lichen and the like for
your pet to take out. Use the 'C' command (or even better a
stethoscope if you are a healer) to note the size of pet until
they get large. Then you are ready to to tackle the mines.

The next aspect is money gathering. You'll need money to pay for
protection. The dungeon is virtually guaranteed to generate at least
one shop in the upper 4 levels. Take the time to clean it out. Use
your pet. Depending on your moral stance, the process of credit
cloning in the shops is a good way to get it done efficiently. Many
players believe that it's cheating. But everything about shops is
a game IMHO. Shopkeeps are trying to separate you from your money,
so why not do the same to them? Note that unidentified gems are
virtually worthless because you can't get more than a nickel for 
them even if they are valuable.

If you note that a vault is on a level (i.e. "You hear the footsteps
or guards on patrol/the counting of money.") then it may be worth the
time to find the vault and clean it out. But to do this you absolutely
need a way out without guard assistance, as the guard will make you 
leave all your money in the vault. A pickaxe is very useful. Often
you can pick one up off a dwarf on the 1st mine level. It may be worth
the effort to see if you can get the pet to separate a dwarf from his
stuff so you can get a pickaxe or broadpick.

Next up are two defensive tactics. The first is that you can get HPs
without gaining XLs. The safest way if you have access is by drinking
potions of healing. If you drink a blessed potion of full healing you
can bump your max HP by 8 points! Of course getting access to such
potions in the early game is tough to pull off.

The more risky method is that your god will bump your max HP up by 
as many as 5 HP under 3 conditions:

1) Your current max HP is at or below 10+XL*5. So 15 for XL1 and 20
for XL2.

2) Your actual HP is below 6.

3) You can safely pray.

I use this tactic by getting next to a newt and letting is bash my HP
down to 5 or 4. I then walk away and pray.

You can combine all these techniques and be XL1 with 20 or 21 HP.

Next up is armor. In this strategy armor is your friend. You should
try on every piece of non cursed armor and wear the best. Your pet
can help with cursed/not cursed because they will avoid cursed items.
My tactic here is to find a straight hallway with a door or boulder
at the end. Trap the pet between you and the door/boulder and drop
armor (and weapons too. sharp edged weapons can be used to force open
locked boxes/chests) and see if the pet will step on it. If they do
without comment (no reluctantly message) or pick it up, then it's
not cursed. Name it as such then wear/wield it. Also make sure you
have a hat on going into the Mines.


>does require any and all tactics in order to get spooks off your case
>(especially those that can take out a XL1).

You've seen my outline above. The most important tactic is RUN!!! If you
max out your HP and armor (but don't burden yourself) you can usually
run away from most anything early in the dungeon. Run to your pet and let
the pet take out the nasty.

Another useful Elbereth tool is a hard gemstone if you can find one.
When you use one to engrave, it'll say "engrave" instead of "write in
the dust". Also it's more permanent that dust writing which often get
smudged just standing there.

Use traps to your advantage. Traps that kill monsters don't count
for XPs. So use pits and falling rock traps to kill stuff.

But at the end of the day I rarely bother with Elbereth or traps. My
main strategy is to run monsters into my pet. It helps buff the pet
and requires little prep. I also armor up and HP up to make it tougher
for a monster to take me out. Also note that your god will restore you
to full HP if you are below 5 HP and can pray. It's a good emergency
full heal if you're really in trouble.

Hope this helps you in your quest. Don't think of the protection racket
as a fast way to get to the mines. There doesn't have to be anything
fast about it. I usually take about 4000 turns prepping for the minetown
run. I clean out all the shops, buff the pet, clean out the vaults,
price ID what I can, bump up the HP, and search for armor all before
making the Minetown run. Also I don't worry about XLs too much. In my
current game I lost my pet early on. So I hung around until the RNG
saw fit to generate another dog/cat. Actually it generated one of each.
I tamed them with food rations and now they are my new pet army. The
upshot is that my character is XL9! I have about $10000 to spend and I
haven't gotten to the minetown shops yet. I figure I can buy about
5 to 6 points of protection and keep moving until I get more money.
The point is that there's no point in staying XL1 if it'll get your
character killed. If you need to get to XL2 or XL3 to be safer for
the run, then you're better off doing that. Simply avoid killing monsters
until you get your protection.

Then all bets are off!

